      "text": "24 Rasm refers to the consonantal skeleton of the Qurʾānic text, lacking vowel signs (ḥarakāt)\nand originally written with minimal or no diacritical dots. Early Qurʾānic manuscripts attest\nto this rasm as the stable textual base upon which later vocalization and qirāʾāt traditions\nwere applied.\n25 Adrian Alan Brockett, Studies in Two Transmissions of the Qurʾān (Stuttgart: Institut für\nOrientalistik der Universität Stuttgart, 1986), which documents the nature of early Qurʾānic\ntransmission and shows that recognized qirāʾāt reflect controlled orthographic and phonetic\nvariation rather than doctrinal divergence.\n26 Don Tapscott and Alex Tapscott, Blockchain Revolution: How the Technology Behind\nBitcoin Is Changing Money, Business, and the World (New York: Portfolio/Penguin, 2016).\nThe authors explain how distributed ledgers achieve tamper-resistance through decentralized\nverification, redundancy, and network-wide consensus—an analogy used here to illustrate\nthe Qurʾān’s distributed preservation through memorization rather than to suggest a\ntechnological equivalence.\n                                49",
