      "text": "Preservation of Revelation\nTopkapı Sarayı Medina 1a (M1) (8th – early 9th CE)\nOriginally preserved in the Prophet’s Mosque in Medina, it was moved to Istanbul\nduring World War I. It is arguably the earliest surviving virtually complete physical\ncopy of the Qur'an, comprising 391 folios written by multiple scribes. Its\northographic variants preserve evidence of early regional written transmission. Only\na couple of later replacement folios exist (e.g., folios 24 and 33 in Surah Āl ʿImrān),\nwhile the original quires33 preserve the full text from al-Fātiḥah through al-Nās.\nAlthough it is not carbon dated, since physical destructive sampling has not been\npermitted by Turkish authorities, the dating has been established via paleography\nand codicology.34\n\nCodex Mashhad (MS 18 & MS 4116) (Late 7th CE)\nDated palaeographically to the 1st century AH. Notably, it preserves evidence of an\nunderlying surah arrangement that originally aligned with the order recorded for\nAbdallāh ibn Masʿūd, which was subsequently emended to conform to the standard\nʿUthmānic surah order. The rearrangements were strictly at the macro-level of entire\nchapters (surahs), not individual verses (āyāt). Codex Mashhad demonstrates that\nthe standard ʿUthmānic wording was already being reproduced in an alternative\nsurah arrangement before the manuscript was reorganized to match the standard\nsequence. It consists of 251 folios preserving over 90% of the Qur'an.35",
