      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\nTopkapi (Istanbul) codex (Late 8th - early 9th CE)\nThe Topkapi manuscript preserves nearly the entire Qurʾānic text, with only minor\nlacunae due to missing folios. Its consonantal framework belongs to the standard\nʿUthmānic textual tradition. Although the manuscript contains scribal and\northographic differences, along with some documented textual variants, it preserves\nremarkably close correspondence with the Qur'an transmitted today. It does not\ndiverge in meaning from the standard Qurʾān recited today supporting the claim of\nlong-term semantic stability.36\n\nSamarkand (Tashkent) codex\nHistorically preserved in Samarkand, this manuscript is now housed in Tashkent,\nUzbekistan.37 Radiocarbon testing and palaeographic analysis place its creation\nbetween the late 8th century and early 9th century CE (Abbasid period), roughly a\ncentury after Caliph 'Uthman's death. It is a partial text (accounting for roughly 30–\n35% of the Quran) consisting of around 250 folios38 that begin midway through\nSurah al-Baqarah (Q 2:7) and end near Surah as-Saffat (Q 37:143). Like other early\nmanuscripts, it lacks vowel markers and diacritical dots on most letters. Although\nthere are lacunae due to lost leaves, its underlying consonantal text (the rasm) shows\nno textual divergence from the canonical 'Uthmanic tradition.\n\nCodex Parisino-petropolitanus (Late 7th – early 8th CE)\nThe Codex Parisino-petropolitanus is one of the oldest surviving manuscripts of the\nQur’an, dating back to the late 7th to early 8th century CE (the Umayyad period).\nThe surviving dispersed folios account for roughly 45% of the Qur'anic text. It was",
