      "text": "Preservation of Revelation\nBirmingham Manuscript (UK) (7th CE)\nThe Birmingham Manuscript (UK),44 radiocarbon dated to 568–645 CE (95.4%\nprobability), is a Mingana 1572a parchment, which means it overlaps with the\nProphet’s lifetime. While the dating applies to the writing material rather than the\nink, the manuscript nonetheless constitutes one of the earliest extant witnesses to the\nQurʾānic text. The specimen contains a very small fragment consisting of 2 folios\n(parts of sūrahs 18, 19, 20) and matches the standard Qurʾānic text at the\nconsonantal level. The manuscript's extraordinary radiocarbon result became public\nin 2015 and recent research points to it being linked to the Paris BnF Arabe 328c.\n\nParis BnF Arabe 328c (France) (7th CE)\nThe Paris BnF Arabe 328c is a crucial 1st-century Hijri (7th century CE) Qurʾānic\nmanuscript fragment acquired from Egypt by a French collector in the 19th century.\nIt is written on parchment and features minimal diacritical dots, and no vowel\nmarks. While closely associated with the overall BnF Arabe 328 collection (which\nincludes the Codex Parisino-Petropolitanus, shelfmark 328a), Arabe 328c represents\na distinct, separate manuscript codex.\nCodicological analysis by scholar Alba Fedeli demonstrated that the 16 folios of BnF\nArabe 328c belong to the exact same original codex as the two famous folios of the\nBirmingham Quran manuscript (Mingana 1572a). Because it is part of the same\nmanuscript as the Birmingham leaves, radiocarbon dating applies to the combined\nmanuscript makes this one of the oldest surviving physical fragments of the Quran\nin the world. Together, the sixteen folios in Paris and two folios in Birmingham (18\nfolios total) preserve roughly 8.3% of the Quranic text. They are virtually identical to\n\n44 Alba Fedeli, “The Birmingham Qurʾān Manuscript,” Journal of Islamic Manuscripts 9\n(2018): 1–24.\n\n                                60",
