      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\n\n     Qurʾānic Principle          Philosophical Parallel                Implication\n Assumption has no truth-      Epistemic justification       Truth requires evidence, not\n value (Q 10:36; 53:28; 45:32)   theory                         intuition\n Following conjecture is         Ethics of belief                Responsibility extends to how\n blameworthy                                                       beliefs are formed\n Most people follow            Cognitive psychology        Confirmation bias and heuristics\n assumption                                                 predate modern science\n Knowledge must replace       Rational epistemology         Feeling certain ≠ being right\n  intuition\n\n4.  Experiential and Modern Corroboration\n   Modern life confirms the Qurʾān’s diagnosis: False narratives spread through\nassumption. Misinformation thrives on rapid, unverified belief. Social media\namplifies “viral” news over evidence. People confuse probability with certainty.\nEmotional resonance often overrides factual reality. Every cognitive bias — from\nheuristics to groupthink — shows how unreliable assumption is.\n   The Qurʾān identified this failure long before psychology formalized it. Whereas\nmodern society now grapples with the dangers of assumption, the Qurʾān warned\nagainst it from the beginning.\n\n5.  Conclusion: The Qurʾānic Rejection of Guesswork\n   The Qurʾān demands a clean epistemic environment. Belief must be formed\nthrough knowledge, not feeling; reflection, not intuition; verification, not cultural\nhabit. By stripping assumption of legitimacy, the Qurʾān purifies the path to truth.\nNo fabricated system attacks the psychological mechanisms that make persuasion\nsimple.\n   Only a text anchored in truth can afford to say: Assumption is worthless.\nConjecture cannot guide you. Most people follow what has no epistemic weight.\nHuman mass psychology thrives on assumption—contrary to how divine guidance\nfunctions.",
