      "text": "Appendix E: Al-Kitāb: Recited and written from the very\nbeginning.\n   Here we provide internal evidence from the Qurʾān that Prophet Muhammad\nwas literate and engaged with its written form since inception.\n\nThe Qurʾān’s Definition of Ummī\nQ 2:78 — The defining verse\n         يَظُنُّونَ                إَِّلا هُمْ  إِنْوَ  أَمَانِيَّ  إَِّلا الْكِتَابَ يَعْلَمُونَ  َلا  ِّيُّونَأُم  مِنْهُمْوَ\n   Wa-min-hum ʾummiyyūn lā yaʿlamūna al-kitāba illā ʾamāniyya wa-in\n   hum illā yaẓunnūn.\n    “And among them are unlettered (ummiyyūn) ones who do not know the\n     Book, except wishful thinking; they only assume.” (Q 2:78)\n    Here the self-referential Qurʾān itself defines ʾummiyyūn as “those who do not\nknow the Book.” Their deficiency lies in ignorance of revelation, not in the inability\nto read or write. They replace revealed knowledge with ʾamānī (“wishful thinking”)\nand ẓann (“assumptions”).\n    The word ʾUmmī ( ِّّيأُم ) derives from root hamza mīm mīm ( م م أ )— “source,\nmother, community”—following the fuʿʿī pattern, which denotes belonging. Thus,\nʾummī literally means “one from among the people,” or “one of the common,\nunscriptured folk.”\n\nOther Qurʾānic Uses of Ummī and Ummiyyūn\n\n   The word ʾummī occur in the Qurʾān six times, four times as a noun (Q 2:78, Q\n3:20, Q 3:75, Q 62:2) and twice as an adjective (Q 7:157-158).\n    Let’s look at the remaining five occurrences in the table below. The word\nʾummiyyīn is contrasted with the people of the book in the Qurʾān. Ahl al-Kitāb =\npeople who possess earlier scripture. ʾUmmiyyūn = people who do not.",
