      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\n\nTable E1\nOccurrences of the term Ummī in the Qurʾān.\n\n   Verse               Phrase / Context                 Description and Semantic Function\n\n                                                     Contrasts those given the Book (Ahl al-\n           “…wa-qul lilladhīna ūtū al-kitāba wa-    Kitāb) with al-ʾummiyyīn—the\n Q 3:20\n            al-ʾummiyyīn…”                         unscriptured peoples lacking prior\n                                                            revelation.\n\n                                                           Explicit mention of al-ʾummiyyīn (“those\n                                                  without prior scripture”); contrasts Ahl al-\n            “…dhālika bi-annahum qālū laysa\n Q 3:75                                               Kitāb’s in-group ethics toward the\n             ʿalaynā fī al-ʾummiyyīna sabīl…”\n                                               ʾummiyyūn, reinforcing that ʾummiyyūn ≠\n                                                                       illiterate, but “non-scriptured others.\n\n             “…al-nabiyy al-ʾummī…”                 Refers to “the Prophet from among the\n Q 7:157–\n             “…wa-rasūlihi al-nabiyyi al-            unscriptured”—one raised without earlier\n 158\n           ʾummiyy…”                                scripture who becomes its first bearer.\n\n                                                      Describes a Messenger raised among the\n          “Huwa alladhī baʿatha fī al-ʾummiyyīn\n Q 62:2                                             unscriptured nation, tasked with reciting,\n             rasūlan minhum…”\n                                                         purifying, and teaching the Book.\n\n    Thus, when the Qurʾān calls Prophet Muhammad al-nabiyy al-ʾummī (Q\n7:157), it simply means: “The Prophet from a people without previous revelation.”\nBefore revelation, he shared their ʾummiyyah; afterwards, he became teacher of the\nBook (Q 62:2).",
