      "topics": [],
      "text": "We are thoroughly acquainted by now with the concept of elided events, having been able both \nto identify them and supply their details in the cases of Hūd and Abraham as well as from our \nstudy of various kinds of narrative summary. While Lot does not expressly address himself to \nthe ruling elite (‘the eminent ones’ in the text) in the segments below, we know that such was \nthe case on the basis of what we saw in Chapter 8.2\n\nIn summary, then, local specifics and emphasis notwithstanding, the narrative is standard: a \nman warns the community to which he belongs; rejection of that man’s message by the people \nto which he was sent, and that community’s subsequent destruction at the hand of God.\n\nIn this chapter we are primarily interested in explicit features; all features not explicitly \nmentioned which are covered by generic narrative summaries must be assumed to have",
